References:
    https://blog.ashutoshkrris.in/the-ultimate-guide-to-the-java-singleton-pattern
    

Singleton Pattern : A class that has only one instance, and provieds global access
to that instance. 
    1. Private Constructor : private SingleonClass() {}
    2. Private Instance : private static instance;
    3. Public method : public static getInstance () { return instance; }

1. Eager Singleton
2. Lazy Singleton
3. Synchronized Method Singleton
    With this, even after the first instance created, all the other threads / calls
    will wait to be exuecuted in serial fashion, cuncorrency missing
4. Double Checked Locking Singleton (Static Block Synchronized Singleton with 
    volatile keyword)

5. Inner Class Singleton

For all these approaches, we have a problem
    a. Reflection : using reflection, a developer can access the private Constructor
        Constructors[] constructors = Singleton.class.getDeclaredConstructors();
        for (Constructor constructor : constructors){
            constructor.setAccessible(true);
            instanceTwo = (Singleton) constructor.newInstance();
            break;
        }

        We may need to add a check in the constructor 
        private Singleton () {
            if (instance != null){
                throw new RuntimeException("Instance already exists! use getInstance()
                method");
            }
        }

        Note : Thought about why cant we instantiate the instance object inside the
            private constructor with a check. But this is wrong, as we are calling
            new Singleton(), which means we are calling the same class initiation
            inside the another initiation. This will cause recussion and endless
            loop and crashes the program.


    b. Serialization
    c. Cloning



6. Final Better Indestructable Solutoin : Enum
        public enum SingletonEnum {
            INSTANCE;

            public getInstance() {
                // your config code to db
            }
        }

    In Main Method
        SingletonEnum.INSTANCE.getInstance();


    // Enums
    // Multiple variables pointing to the same enum instance
        public enum Status {
            PENDING;
        }
        Status order1 = Status.PENDING;
        Status order2 = Status.PENDING; 

        System.out.println(order1 == order2); // Prints: true

